{
  "requirement": "The `PlainText` implementation of `OutlineLang` supplies the extension, selection, and fingerprint rules described in this chapter.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub struct PlainText;\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for PlainText;\nimpl Debug for PlainText;\nimpl Default for PlainText;\nimpl OutlineLang for PlainText;\nimpl Copy for PlainText;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.sdk.sources.plain-text.PlainText` declares the unit struct `PlainText`. It declares the trait impls `Clone`, `Debug`, `Default`, `Copy` and `OutlineLang`. It also says the type supplies the extension, selection and fingerprint rules of the chapter. The detailed item and fingerprint rules belong to `requirements.sdk.sources.plain-text.items` and `requirements.sdk.sources.plain-text.fingerprints`. Those are separate requirements with their own tests, so I only checked that the `OutlineLang` implementation is exercised.\n\n**Extension rule.**\n- `bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline` asserts `plain.name() == \"plain-text\"` and `plain.extensions().is_empty()`. So `PlainText` claims no extensions.\n- `unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors` shows the fallback. Files named `missing.unclaimed` and `README` have no claiming language, so they get plain-text outlines (root plus `line` items). A file with no extension, `missing`, also gets the fallback outline, with a single empty root item for empty content.\n\n**Selection rule.**\n- The same test checks that the root selector is `\"\"` and that the root span is `Span::new(0, 18)`.\n- It checks that blank lines are skipped and that the line spans are `alpha`, `beta`, `alpha`.\n- It checks that the `line@<12 hex>` selectors match `structural_hash[..12]`.\n- It checks that duplicate lines share a selector and that distinct lines do not.\n- It checks that `select` returns the right text.\n\n**Fingerprint rule.**\n- `plain_line_fingerprints_use_sha256_words_without_losing_boundaries` asserts a known SHA-256 value for `hello`. It asserts that whitespace differences are normalised away and that word boundaries are kept (`two words` is not equal to `twowords`). It asserts the scheme `plain-text-structural-v1`.\n- `unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors` asserts that reflowing the whitespace leaves the whole-content fingerprint unchanged. It asserts that reordering or joining lines changes it.\n\n**`OutlineLang` behaviour.** `outline_only_languages_report_unsupported_structural_search` asserts that `PlainText.search_structure` returns an error mentioning `plain-text`. The outline, name, extensions and fingerprint scheme are covered by the tests above.\n\n**Derived traits.** The `language!` macro in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/languages/mod.rs` generates the struct with `#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default)]`. These derives have no conditionals or transformations of their own, so they fall under the testing exemption for code that only passes values through. Using `languages::PlainText` as a value in the tests also relies on the unit struct and `Copy`. The shared macro is exercised for the other languages through `check`'s `Clone`, `Default` and `Copy` assertions.\n\n**Marking.** The three relevant tests carry `{test}` references to `PlainText`. I found no gap.",
